course: dictionary
lesson: 13816
title: A formal word: irrespective
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words belong to rules, notices and exam papers. Irrespective is one of them. It means the thing named makes no difference, so you do not take it into account. «All pupils may enter, irrespective of age.» Whatever your age is, you may enter. Age does not change the answer.
explain: Irrespective is often followed by of, or by whether. «The sports day goes ahead, irrespective of the weather.» Here the weather does not change the decision. «We will play, irrespective of whether it rains.» Whether it rains or not, the game still happens.
explain: A good check is to swap in a simpler phrase. "No matter" or "whatever" often fits. «Irrespective of the cost» becomes «no matter the cost». If your swapped version still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Irrespective means without taking something into account; it makes no difference.
fact: «Irrespective of» is followed by a noun, as in «irrespective of age» or «irrespective of money».
fact: «Irrespective of whether» is followed by a clause, as in «irrespective of whether it is raining».
fact: Irrespective is a formal word, so you will meet it most in rules, notices and exam papers.
fact: A simple swap is «no matter»: «irrespective of age» means «no matter how old you are».
exercise: «All pupils may enter, irrespective of age.» What does irrespective of age mean? | Only the older children may enter | Children must be exactly ten to enter | *Age makes no difference, so anyone may enter | Nobody is allowed in
exercise: Which phrase means the same as «irrespective of the weather»? | *No matter what the weather is | Before the weather changes | Because of the weather | Only when the weather is good
exercise: Which sentence shows that money does not change the result? | Irrespective, money is the only thing that matters. | *Entry is the same for all, irrespective of how much money you have. | Entry is free only when you bring money. | Entry costs more for rich people, because of their money.
test: «The library is open to everyone, irrespective of where they live.» What does irrespective mean here? | Everyone must move house first | Only people who live nearby can use it | The library closes at night | *It does not matter where they live
test: Which phrase best replaces irrespective of in «Irrespective of the cost, we will buy the tickets.»? | Only if the cost is low | Because of the cost | Before the cost is known | *No matter what the cost is
exam: «Irrespective of whether you win or lose, the team shakes hands.» What does this sentence tell us? | Winning is the only reason to shake hands | The team never shakes hands | The team shakes hands only if it wins | *The handshake happens whether they win or lose
